# Sources: Fakhreddin Azimi, Iran: The Crisis of Democracy, 1941–1953,
# pp. 207–208; Ervand Abrahamian, Iran Between Two Revolutions, ch. 5,
# pp. 250–267. The three structures are counterfactual alternatives.
title: The <span class="term term-national-front">National Front</span>'s Structure
subtitle: What kind of coalition can hold unlike organizations together?
new-page: true
tags: event
view-if: year = 1949 and month >= 11 and front_formed = 0
max-visits: 1
priority: 20
on-arrival: front_formed = 1

= November 1949

The cooperation organized around
{!<span class="term term-mossadegh">!}Mohammad Mossadegh{!</span>!} and the
protest against the Sixteenth
{!<span class="term term-parliament">!}Majles{!</span>!} election is becoming
the {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}National Front{!</span>!}.
Representative elections, constitutional government, and political liberties
provide common ground. They do not make the participants one party.

The coalition includes established party organizations, {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}independent
nationalists{!</span>!}, and religious-political networks. Each brings people and access
the others lack; each also has reasons to resist discipline by the whole. A
constitution for the {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} would therefore distribute more than offices. It
would decide how much autonomy its components surrender before they have
learned to trust one another.

- @narrow_program
- @coordination_committee
- @membership_front

@narrow_program
title: Adopt a narrow constitutional program
subtitle: A short pledge lets unlike organizations stand together without building a common apparatus
on-arrival: front_structure = "Narrow constitutional alliance"; constitutional_legitimacy += 5; iran_party_relation += 3; independent_nationalists_relation += 3

The {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} publishes a compact basis for cooperation: representative elections,
constitutional government, and political freedoms. Because the program does
not demand agreement on every social or religious question, the {!<span class="term term-constitutionalist">!}Iran Party{!</span>!} and
independent constitutional politicians can defend it without disguising their
differences.

The clarity gives the alliance a strong public rule. It leaves local
membership and discipline largely in the hands of its component organizations,
so later coordination will still have to be negotiated.

- @post_event: Continue

@coordination_committee
title: Remain a committee of cooperating organizations
subtitle: Each organization retains its own command while the committee brokers agreement
on-arrival: front_structure = "Loose coordination committee"; iran_party_relation += 1; toilers_relation += 1; independent_nationalists_relation += 1; religious_network_relation += 1; iran_party_dissent -= 2; toilers_dissent -= 2; independent_nationalists_dissent -= 2; religious_network_dissent -= 2

Each organization keeps its own membership and leadership while sending
representatives to a common coordinating table. Reports move more easily
between circles, and nobody must pretend that the {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} can command supporters
it did not recruit.

The arrangement lowers the immediate cost of cooperation. It also means that a
future disagreement can travel back through several independent chains of
authority before the {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} can answer as one body.

- @post_event: Continue

@membership_front
title: Build a common membership organization
subtitle: Direct membership gives the <span class="term term-national-front">Front</span> machinery of its own—and raises the question of who controls it
on-arrival: front_structure = "Membership-based coalition"; iran_party_organization += 2; toilers_organization += 2; independent_nationalists_organization += 3; religious_network_organization += 2; iran_party_dissent += 2; toilers_dissent += 2; religious_network_dissent += 3

The coalition begins enrolling supporters through common local committees
rather than relying only on the rolls of its component groups. {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Independent
nationalists{!</span>!} gain machinery they previously lacked, and the {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} acquires a
more direct route from leadership decisions to local work.

Existing organizations immediately ask who controls those committees and
whose program new members are joining. The stronger common structure is real;
so is the resistance from allies who fear being absorbed by it.

- @post_event: Continue
